<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iwate Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iwate Prefecture is a prefecture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku region</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morioka">Morioka</a>. It is the second-largest prefecture by area, mountainous and thinly populated, with the Kitakami river valley and the rugged Sanriku coast. It was formed after the abolition of the domains in 1871 largely from the lands of the Morioka domain, and its coast was devastated by the tsunami of March 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iwate-prefecture.jpg" alt="Iwate Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 掬茶 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cherry_tree_and_Mount_Iwate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Iwate faces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> on the east, with sheer rocky cliffs along most of the shore. The Ōu Mountains on the west, where Mount Iwate, at 2,038 metres the highest point of the prefecture, is still an active volcano, form the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akita-prefecture">Akita Prefecture</a>. The older Kitakami Mountains run through the middle from north to south, and between the two ranges flows the Kitakami River, the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku</a>. Its large, fertile basin holds the prefecture's biggest cities and farms. In the past Iwate was known for gold, iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and sulfur, which are no longer produced.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jōmon people inhabited the area, leaving burial pits at Nishida and clay figurines, masks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> at sites such as Tateishi, Makumae and Kunenbashi. The Yamato government claimed all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> from the earliest times, but its forces could not occupy any part of what became Iwate until 802. In March 787 the Emishi leaders, among them Aterui, trapped the Yamato infantry at Sufuse Village and pushed them into the Kitakami River, where more than 1,000 soldiers drowned in their heavy armour.</p><p>When battle failed, the Japanese made the Emishi dependent on trade for iron wares and sake, bribed leaders with rank and citizenship, and burned crops and relocated Emishi women and children to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. On 15 April 802 More and Aterui surrendered with about 500 warriors. They were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and beheaded against the wishes of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakanoue-no-tamuramaro">Sakanoue no Tamuramaro</a>, and the Emishi fought on for twenty years or more. Forts on the Chinese model were built along the Kitakami. In the latter part of the Heian period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a> became the capital of the Northern Fujiwara, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoshitsune">Minamoto no Yoshitsune</a> fled there after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a>. Until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> the area belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu Province</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Iwate is the second-largest prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, and its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morioka">Morioka</a>. It has the lowest population density of any prefecture outside Hokkaido. Its sights include Morioka Castle and the Buddhist temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuson-ji">Chūson-ji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motsu-ji">Mōtsū-ji</a>. The poet Matsuo Bashō visited Hiraizumi and wrote of it in Oku no Hosomichi. The coast has suffered repeated earthquakes and tsunamis, among them the tsunami of 15 June 1896, whose waves reached 24 metres at Yoshihama and killed 18,158 people in Iwate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iwate Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After abolition in 1871 it became part of <b>Iwate Prefecture</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/morioka-domain">Morioka Domain</a></li></ul></section>
